            <ArticleTitle>A national framework for supporting improvements in the physiotherapy assessment and management of low back pain: the Scottish experience.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>198-205</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.physio.2010.02.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Similar to other countries worldwide, Scotland lacked a national view of whether the quality of the physiotherapy management of low back pain was compliant with national guidelines. Anecdotal evidence suggested that standards of care varied considerably despite the wide availability of clinical guidelines to clinicians.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To develop a framework that supports National Health Service (NHS) Scotland in providing consistently applied high-quality physiotherapy assessment and management of low back pain in line with guideline recommendations.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, multicentred national study, data collection and improvement phase.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">All NHS boards in Scotland (n=14) plus two private provider sites.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">One hundred and eighty-six individual NHS sites and two private providers of services to patients with low back pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">A national dataset was developed from evidence- and consensus-based guideline sources. All sites collected data (two 5-week periods) over 1 year (2008-2009) using a web-based database. This was interspersed by an improvement phase during which required improvements were considered and implemented. Issues were shared through a national network and national meeting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Data from 2147 patients showed improvements in the documented physiotherapy management of low back pain over the two cycles. All participants developed and implemented remedial action plans based on the results of the first cycle.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">It is possible to implement a framework, which is led nationally but driven and owned locally, supporting physiotherapists in an active programme of locally determined improvement. However, although process and outcome are linked, the direct impact of this initiative on patient outcome is not known.</AbstractText>
